abstract: >-
  Adding context to a prompt changes two things at once: the information
  it carries and the format it arrives in. Comparing augmented against
  unaugmented cannot separate them, so a reported improvement is
  compatible with either explanation. This repository implements a
  format-matched control - a third arm identical in shape to the augmented
  one, with the values permuted between entities so that the mapping from
  entity to fact is destroyed while row count, field names, ordering and
  token count are preserved. The difference between the augmented and
  permuted arms isolates the information component.

  The worked example is SVG element identification. Thirty generated SVGs
  each contain several elements sharing a tag and fill whose path geometry
  is replaced by fixed-length opaque tokens, so position and size cannot be
  read from the source. Instructions name exactly one element by spatial
  position or ordinal size, verified unique under every reasonable
  operationalisation. The corpus, scoring rules, predicates and analysis
  plan were frozen and content-addressed before any model output was
  observed.

  The measured outcome is a constrained null. All three arms scored
  identification accuracy 0.0444, against a random-selection reference of
  0.1852, with every pairwise difference 0.0000 and a minimum detectable
  effect of 0.0289. The context demonstrably reached the model and altered
  56 of 180 responses, but did not change which element was identified.
  Because the treatment effect was zero, the decomposition the control was
  built for had no quantity to operate on - a limitation the artifact
  states rather than obscures.
